{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"3d15a92c-eb9b-4002-a3e6-a447f1ed53f6","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I have completed the `lab-17-recoverable-memory` setup as requested.\n\nHere is a summary of the work done:\n- **LRVM Implementation:** Implemented a lightweight recoverable virtual memory library in C (`src/rvm.c` and `src/rvm.h`) that maps memory privately, creates undo records via `set_range`, appends redo records to a log, handles crash recovery by applying the log, and implements log truncation.\n- **Testing and Crash Injection:** Created `src/test_rvm.c` that validates aborts, commits, truncations, and uses `fork()` and `kill -9` to simulate crashes and verify rollback of uncommitted changes.\n- **Rio Vista Motivation:** Added an `fio` benchmark (`fio-fsync.sh`) to demonstrate the high latency of synchronous disk `fsync` calls compared to memory operations, motivating the Rio Vista concept.\n- **Infrastructure:** Set up the `Makefile` with `all`, `run`, `test`, and `clean` targets. Suppressed all compiler warnings and ignored binary/output files using `.gitignore`.\n- **Validation:** Successfully ran the tests and benchmarks in the `aos` Lima VM using `run-in-vm.sh test` and safely collected the output to `expected-output.txt` using `run-in-vm.sh capture`.\n- **Documentation:** Updated the `README.md` to `status: solid`, integrating explanations of how the code works, linking to the relevant L08a and L08b concepts, and adding structured experiments and folded Q\u0026A.\n- **Commit:** Added and committed all changes to the requested target branch using a conventional commit message.\n- **Report:** Saved the requested task report detailing the actions and verifications to `/Users/shreejitverma/github/career-ops/library/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/CS6210-AOS/crew/runner/reports/lab-17-recoverable-memory.md`.\n","duration_seconds":196.956616,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":114154,"output_tokens":17090,"thinking_tokens":5946,"cache_read_